-- title:
--   Antipode fixes the finite-part idempotent
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring that is local, and let $H$ be a commutative ring carrying the structure of a Hopf algebra over $R$; write $S =$ `HopfAlgebra.antipode R` for its antipode, an $R$-linear endomorphism of $H$. Let $e \in H$ be an idempotent element, $e^2 = e$. Two hypotheses are imposed on the pair of complementary corners cut out by $e$: first, the localisation of $H$ away from $e$, that is $H[1/e]$, is finite as an $R$-module; second, the extension of the maximal ideal $\mathfrak m$ of $R$ along the structure map $R \to H[1/(1-e)]$, the localisation of $H$ away from $1 - e$, is the unit ideal, i.e. $\mathfrak m \, H[1/(1-e)] = H[1/(1-e)]$ (equivalently, the corner complementary to $e$ has empty closed fibre, and in particular is the zero ring when its structure map is not injective on no further assumption). Under these hypotheses the conclusion is the single equation $S(e) = e$: the antipode fixes $e$. No flatness, Noetherian or Henselian assumption on $R$ or $H$ is required.
--
--   Geometrically this says that the inversion map of the affine group scheme $\operatorname{Spec} H$ over a local base preserves the open-and-closed "finite part" cut out by $e$, namely the part that is module-finite over $R$, its complement having empty special fibre. It is the Hopf-algebra ingredient used by [`HopfAlgebra.exists_hopfAlgebra_bialgHom_surjective_ker_eq_span_one_sub_of_finitePartIdempotent`](thm.html#HopfAlgebra.exists_hopfAlgebra_bialgHom_surjective_ker_eq_span_one_sub_of_finitePartIdempotent), which produces from such an idempotent a surjective bialgebra homomorphism onto a Hopf algebra with kernel the ideal generated by $1 - e$, i.e. the finite part as a group scheme in its own right.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_HopfAlgebra_antipode_finitePartIdempotent.lean

